    "effectivePlan" : "Aim for nine tricks: four diamonds after conceding the ace, three hearts, and the two black aces. Play low from dummy now and normally duck the first two spade rounds, winning the third with the ace. This can exhaust an opponent's three-card spade holding before that opponent gains the diamond ace. After gaining the lead, attack diamonds immediately and continue until the ace is dislodged. Preserve dummy's heart ace as an entry to any stranded diamond winners; cash South's heart honors before using that entry when practical. Retain the club ace against a defensive switch. If defenders change suits before the third spade, reassess the hold-up while prioritizing diamond establishment. The principal danger is the diamond ace accompanying long spades; the hold-up cannot overcome every such layout, but protects against the important long-spade\/short-spade split when the short hand holds the ace.",
